  <p>
    Across all 16 metros, architect intent adds up to 14,110 searches a month. The adjacent trades
    add up to 102,880. The median metro sends almost nine times more people looking for a builder
    or a remodeler than for the person who could design the thing properly. Part of that is an
    October surge in contractor searches covered further down. Leave October out and the median is
    still above seven to one.
  </p>

  <h2>Your market is searching. It is searching for someone else</h2>
  <p>
    That ratio is the most useful column in the table, because it puts a number on something firms
    already suspect. Chicago produces 1,130 architect searches a month and 3,600 for kitchen
    remodeling alone. In Denver the ratio is nearly twelve to one.
  </p>
  <p>
    Those people are not cheap prospects. Somebody searching "custom home builder near me" in Dallas
    has a project, a site and a budget. They have simply decided that a builder is the first phone
    call, because nobody told them a different order exists. That gap is the whole argument in <a href="/referral-systems-for-architects">referral systems for architects</a> and it is why demand you generate beats demand you wait for.
  </p>
  <p>
    Which is also why the map above is not a shopping list of keywords to chase. You can win the 240
    searches in Nashville, and you should, through <a href="/google-business-profile-for-architects">your Google Business Profile</a> and <a href="/seo-for-architects">SEO for architects</a>. It will not fill a calendar. Reaching the other 1,390 people who never type your job title is what paid social is for, and that is covered in <a href="/meta-ads-for-architects">Meta ads for architects</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>The click price has almost nothing to do with the demand</h2>
  <p>
    Sort the table by cost instead of volume and it scrambles. Chicago, the third biggest market,
    has the most expensive click at $19.29. Seattle, with about a quarter of Los Angeles's demand, is second
    at $18.17. Houston has twice Austin's demand and the second cheapest click on the board at $4.57.
  </p>
  <p>
    Google's estimates are thinnest at the bottom of the table. Salt Lake City's $1.52 sits on about
    170 searches a month for "architect near me", while Google's own range for a top-of-page bid
    there runs from $3.80 to $18.82, so read the small metros as a direction rather than a quote. </p>
  <p>
    The spread on the same search intent still says the local auction, not local demand, sets what you
    pay. The trade-off between buying that click and creating your own demand is worked through in <a href="/meta-ads-vs-google-ads-for-architects#the-real-question-capture-versus-create">Meta ads vs Google Ads for architects</a>.
  </p>

