  <p>
    Demand held: 12 of the 16 metros landed within 10% of their July figure, and the total rose from
    13,040 to 14,110. Prices moved: 13 of the 16 clicks changed by more than 20%. Austin went from
    the most expensive click in the set to the third cheapest. Tampa and Nashville, the two cheapest
    in July, both more than doubled. Dallas moved three cents.
  </p>
  <p>
    So a click price you looked up in the summer tells you very little about what you would pay
    this winter. If a firm chose its market, or its channel, from one keyword-tool screenshot, it
    chose on a number that had a shelf life of a few months. Houston and Dallas are the only two
    metros that stayed under $8 in both pulls.
  </p>

  <h2>When people search for an architect during the year</h2>
  <p>
    The November pull also gives a full month-by-month year for each metro, November 2025 to October
    2026. Added across all 16, architect searches bottomed out last winter at 9,800 in November and
    9,520 in December, jumped 54% to 14,650 in January, and peaked at 18,260 in March. </p>
  <p>
    July shows a similar figure, but most of the bump is one term: "custom home architect" spiked to 4,820
    searches that month against roughly 500 in a normal one, and Google's national number shows the
    same one-month spike. Take it out and July is an ordinary 13,390.
  </p>
  <p>
    Which means the next two months are the quietest of the year for people typing your job title,
    and January is when it comes back. </p>
  <p>
    The projects do not disappear in December. The planning
    carries on at the kitchen table and in a developer's spreadsheet, and it shows up as searches
    once the holidays are over. Ads that reach those people before they search are the part of the
    calendar a firm controls, which is the case made in <a href="/meta-ads-for-architects">Meta ads for architects</a>, and the
    budget side of it is in the <a href="/architecture-marketing-budget-calculator">architecture marketing budget calculator</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    The adjacent trades did something stranger. Across the 16 metros their searches went from
    152,330 in September to 287,740 in October, nearly double, led by "kitchen remodeling near me"
    and "general contractor near me". Google's national figures show the same jump, 135,000 to
    301,000 for kitchen remodeling. </p>
  <p>
    Architect searches rose 8% over the same month. We have one
    month of it and no way yet to tell a real autumn rush from a quirk in how Google reports, so it
    sits in this paragraph and not in a headline. The next pull will say which.
  </p>

  <h2>What our own accounts cost in five of these metros</h2>
  <p>
    We run paid social for architecture firms, which means we can put real cost per lead next to
    Google's click prices in the same markets. The accounts are the 21 in our <a href="/architecture-marketing-benchmark-report-2026">benchmark report</a>: $117,656 in spend and 2,419 leads between
    15 September 2025 and 11 September 2026, re-cut by market on 11 October 2026.
  </p>
  <p>
    An account counts for a metro when the service area the firm wrote down before launch is
    centered on it, and when it produced at least 20 leads in the window. That rule moves one
    Miami-based firm into New York, because its ads ran on 25 Long Island ZIP codes. It also leaves
    Austin and Dallas off the table: the Austin firm's ads ran on ZIP codes in six states, and the
    Dallas firm's on all of Texas.
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Metro</th><th>Our accounts</th><th>Our median cost per lead</th><th>Google CPC, July / Nov</th><th>Architect searches / mo</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Miami-Ft. Lauderdale, FL</td><td>1</td><td>$77.63</td><td>$10.63 / $12.17</td><td>500</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Denver, CO</td><td>2</td><td>$73.02</td><td>$13.85 / $10.87</td><td>580</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Tampa-St. Petersburg, FL</td><td>1</td><td>$61.51</td><td>$4.99 / $12.45</td><td>580</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Los Angeles, CA</td><td>2 (1 commercial)</td><td>$57.87</td><td>$14.71 / $11.08</td><td>2,310</td></tr>
      <tr><td>New York, NY</td><td>3</td><td>$53.30</td><td>$8.04 / $10.77</td><td>3,690</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    One to three accounts per metro is an anecdote, so read this as a reason to check your own
    market rather than as a law. </p>
  <p>
    Even so, Google's click price does not predict ours in either pull.
    In July, Los Angeles had the second most expensive click of the five and our second cheapest
    leads, and Tampa had the cheapest click by a distance while sitting in the middle on ours. By
    November all five clicks sat between $10.77 and $12.45, and our medians still span $53.30 to
    $77.63.
  </p>
  <p>
    The sharper finding is inside a single metro. Our three New York accounts ran from $29.86 to
    $64.11 per lead, a factor of 2.1. The five metro medians run from $53.30 to $77.63, a factor of
    1.5. Two firms in the same market differ more than the markets do, so most of what sets a
    firm's cost per lead travels with the firm.
  </p>
  <p>
    Worth saying plainly: a Google click and a Meta lead are different things bought in different
    auctions. Comparing them tells you about the temperature of a market, not about which channel
    to buy.
  </p>

